<<~ aka lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language>>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Minting Over a Retirement

Minting Over a Retirement names a word introduced beside one the graph already ruled.

The node reaches for a term, finds it apt, and seats it — without checking whether the graph retired its synonym and left a residue meme pointing forward. ''The residue carries what gets walked past'': a retired address survives precisely to answer, and answering forward, and the node reads straight through it.

''The instance'': `layer` minted beside `realm`, when `cabal-place` had already retired into `cabal-realm` to stop exactly that blur — and the minting happened ''while writing into that very meme''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ detect the reach for a new word — a name, a primitive, a role, a field.
⏿ orient by ''grepping the graph'', not by recalling it: retirement residues carry `retires-to` in their meta and `RETIRE-RESIDUE` in their role.
◇ decide: a residue found → take the ruled word. None found → the mint may stand.
▶ act, and if the mint stands, check the near-synonyms already in canon before seating it.
↺ if a retirement was walked past, the whole arc downstream carries the blur — sweep it in one pass, never span by span.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

''Before naming a word, grep for what the graph already retired.''

    grep -rn 'retires-to\|RETIRE-RESIDUE' bags/

The node MUST run that check before seating a new term into canon.
The node SHOULD prefer a ruled word over a fresh one even where the fresh one reads more precise — a ruling carries the reason its alternatives lost, and that reason usually still holds.
The node MUST NOT treat a retired address as dead: a residue answers, and answers forward, which is the whole of why it survives.

''The structural cure'': this failure is ''greppable''. It needs no discipline, only one call before naming — which makes it the cheapest of the craft failures to close and the least excusable to repeat.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/diagnostic>>

If a `retires-to` residue names the synonym of a word just minted, Minting Over a Retirement occurred.
If the grep ran and returned nothing, the mint stands clean.
If a ruled word exists with no residue and the node minted anyway, that reads as a different failure — not walking before minting.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

